# Template only — NOT loaded by firebase-tools, which looks for `.env`, `.env.<projectId>` and
# `.env.<alias>`. Copy the block below to the real filename before a prod functions deploy.
#
# ⚠️ PROD IS NOT CONFIGURED AND THE FILE IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT FROM THIS REPO. Setting it is
# Nick's, alongside the deploy it enables — see CLAUDE.md, "Functions — the database is named".
# Until it exists, every function REFUSES TO START on prod rather than writing to a database that
# is not there. That is the intended state, not an outage to be patched around.
#
# ---- functions/.env.gen-lang-client-0801391782 ----
# FIRESTORE_DATABASE_ID=ai-studio-ae82196c-c59e-40b9-b209-9fb02f67ade6
# ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Verified 26 Aug with `firebase firestore:databases:list`:
#   gen-lang-client-0801391782  →  ai-studio-ae82196c-…   ONLY. There is no (default).
#   scriptally-dev              →  (default)              ONLY.

# ══════════════ functions/.env — UNTRACKED, and deliberately so ══════════════
#
# firebase-tools loads `.env` first, then `.env.<projectId>`, then `.env.<alias>`. Anything that
# should not be published goes in the bare `.env`, which .gitignore excludes.
#
# ⚠️ WAITLIST_REPLY_TO IS A PERSONAL ADDRESS AND THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. Committing it to a
# tracked env file would publish it on GitHub permanently — in the working tree and in every clone
# of the history — where address scrapers read it. It is not a secret in the Secret Manager sense
# (and must not be one: `defineSecret` is a DEPLOY-TIME precondition, and this value has to be
# optional), so an untracked file is the right home.
#
#   ---- functions/.env ----
#   WAITLIST_REPLY_TO=<Nick's personal address>
#   WAITLIST_CONTACT_EMAIL=<Nick's personal address>
#   ------------------------
#
# ⚠️ WAITLIST_CONTACT_EMAIL IS THE ADDRESS EACH EMAIL PRINTS IN ITS BODY, and it is separate from
# the reply-to for a reason: a reader cannot SEE a Reply-To header, so a dead one fails invisibly,
# whereas a printed address that bounces is a promise broken in the text of the email. Unset it
# falls back to SUPPORT_EMAIL (hello@scriptally.ink), which is the intended public address and is
# unchanged in code — it is the delivery that is broken, not the address.
#
# ⚠️ WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL: Namecheap forwarding for nick@scriptally.ink is configured and NOT
# delivering, so replies to the from-address are lost — and because the MX record was replaced by
# that forwarding, bounce feedback does not reach Resend either. Nobody would find out. When
# forwarding works, delete this variable and the from-address becomes the reply route again.
#
# Unset is survivable: the transport falls back to the from-address and logs a warning. It never
# throws — a missing reply route must not stop a confirmation going out.
